{
 "gdd": "command-simulation-layer",
 "blocking": [
  "CR-7 binding rebuild (L112) / CR-8 entity IDs (L114) / CR-4 actor row / KRN-BIND-01..04 (L934-L937): [main review, confirmed by creative-director] The rebuild binds bare `(player_id, entity_id)` pairs from every binding source into one table. It treats 'an entity delivered for two different PlayerIds or a duplicate pair' as BINDING_REBUILD_CONFLICT (LOADING -> FAULTED). But CR-8 (L114) says IDs are allocated by each owning block from its own counter and 'the kernel allocates none'. The two MVP sources draw from independent counters: Body's `next_actor_id` and the Emitter's `next_emitter_id` (beam ids). A sibling-GDD grep found no namespace tagging on either side that would prevent this. So single-player PlayerId 1 with avatar actor 1 and beam 1 produces a duplicate pair, and every boot faults on valid data. In VS-B, cross-player numeric overlap trips the 'two PlayerIds' rule. `OWNED_BY_ISSUER` cannot tell an actor from a beam that has the same number either. KRN-BIND-01's fixture uses one source with actors 5 and 9, so it cannot expose the defect. This sits on the start-up path and breaks the death = restart-from-autosave loop, so it is a true blocker. FIX: Key half (b) by `(source block_id, entity_id)`, or require namespace-tagged entity IDs as Layout LY-31 does. State which in CR-7 and the CR-4 `actor` row. Limit the duplicate and conflict checks to the same key. Add an AC: a Body source yielding (1, 1) and an Emitter source yielding (1, 1) bind without conflict, and an OWNED_BY_ISSUER command resolves against the correct source. Correct Pixel-Perfect L135 so it no longer cites a 'kernel-global actor_id'."
 ]
}
